    notifications only ever come from events after a known cursor.
  - [1mClock jumps and sleep.[0m [38;5;153mdue.scan()[39m is keyed by [38;5;153mdue:{taskId}:{dueAt}[39m, so a laptop asleep through a deadline produces
    exactly one late notification, not one per missed tick. Same key means two tabs scanning simultaneously can't
    double-fire — no leader election needed.
  - [1mDuplicate materialization.[0m Notifications are keyed by [38;5;153meventSeq[39m, so replay or a race is a no-op write.
  - [1mStorage unavailable or full[0m (private browsing, quota). Fall back to in-memory, show one banner saying notifications
    won't survive reload, keep working for the session. Never throw into the UI.
  - [1mCorrupt or future-version data.[0m Version check on read; unrecognized version is quarantined under a backup key rather
    than parsed optimistically or silently dropped.
  - [1mUnbounded log growth.[0m Trim to the most recent ~500 events; trimming is safe because of the cursor-reset rule above.

  [1mTesting[0m, given unit tests and no build step: [38;5;153mstore[39m takes an injectable storage adapter and [38;5;153mdue[39m takes an injectable
  clock, so every risky behavior is testable without a browser — matcher dispatch by [38;5;153mkind[39m, [38;5;153meventSeq[39m idempotency, the due
  dedupe key across clock jumps, cursor-reset producing zero toasts, log trim, and quota fallback.

  [1mOne gap I want on the record:[0m you declined e2e, which means cross-tab sync — the mechanism that makes this feature
  meaningful today — has no automated coverage. I can unit-test the cursor/matcher logic that sits under it, but the
  [38;5;153mstorage[39m-event wiring itself will be verified by opening two tabs by hand. That's a reasonable trade for a first
  version; I'd rather name it now than have it read as tested later.

